In this Python code, the binary_search function takes four arguments - arr (the sorted array), low (the starting index of the array), high (the ending index of the array), and x (the element to be searched). The function first checks if the high index is greater than or equal to the low index, which is the base case. If the element is present at the middle of the array, it returns the middle index. If the element is smaller than the middle element, it recursively calls the binary_search function on the left subarray. If the element is larger than the middle element, it recursively calls the binary_search function on the right subarray. If the element is not present in the array, it returns -1.
